Ours.  A CPU-bound thread no longer starves the others.  Python's threads
are Pth's, cooperative: they switch only when the running one blocks or
yields.  A thread computing in Python holds the GIL and never blocks, so
no other thread ran again -- not one whose sleep was over, nor one waiting
for the GIL (its timed wait never got to time out and ask for the GIL).
A timer on user CPU time (setitimer ITIMER_VIRTUAL, SIGVTALRM, every
switch interval, started with the first new thread) marks a switch due;
at its next eval-breaker check the running thread drops the GIL, yields
to Pth and takes it back, when another Python thread exists.  The flag is
part of COMPUTE_EVAL_BREAKER, so a recomputed eval_breaker keeps it.
Amix rounds the timer to its clock (about 30 ms for a 10 ms request);
virtual time does not run while the process is idle.  A program's own
ITIMER_VIRTUAL replaces the switch timer.

